{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/291104-scion-gene-pool","id":291104,"title":"Scion Gene Pool","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-24T00:48:03-07:00","id":291104,"title":"Scion Gene Pool","content":"I'm curious... I know when a deity mates with a mortal, it produces a Scion. But what happens if a deity mates with another deity. Are they born as a god, or do they also start off as unawakened Scions? If the later is so, why would someone with two divine parents be born mortal?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-24T02:48:20-07:00","id":291155,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/291104-scion-gene-pool#post291104\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious... I know when a deity mates with a mortal, it produces a Scion. But what happens if a deity mates with another deity. Are they born as a god, or do they also start off as unawakened Scions? If the later is so, why would someone with two divine parents be born mortal?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> According to most mythoogy: the child produced is a god.<br /> According to Scion canon: It's really ST preference, but the child's probably going to be a god due to the zero amount of mortal makeup in said parents.<br /> <br /> My games have worked around this by saying that the child is born a &quot;lesser god&quot; at Legend 2/3. They're not mortal, they could never be mortal. They could be 'untapped' if someone still wants to go the &quot;I just found out I'm adopted!&quot; route, but they're still never actually mortal.<br /> <br /> Uuuusually in those special-snowflake cases for PCs, or the natural result of the children of PCs, we allow Epics to just be innate without needing a Visitation, but a formal Visitaton is required to start up said Scion path.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-24T04:51:48-07:00","id":291197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/291104-scion-gene-pool#post291104\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious... I know when a deity mates with a mortal, it produces a Scion. But what happens if a deity mates with another deity. Are they born as a god, or do they also start off as unawakened Scions? If the later is so, why would someone with two divine parents be born mortal?</div> </div> </div> </div>According to the spoilers we've got on 2e, they start off as God-born, which covers basically any case where there's no mortal parentage - when two Gods have children, where a God has a child with no other direct parent (as with Zeus and Athena), when two Protogenoi, primordial god-figures, have children (Nyx and Chronos being given as the example parents here - I use the word Protogenoi because we don't know how 2e will classify them)... What being God-born <i>means</i> exactly we don't know yet.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-24T12:08:13-07:00","id":291480,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aynie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/291104-scion-gene-pool?p=291155#post291155\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My games have worked around this by saying that the child is born a &quot;lesser god&quot; at Legend 2/3. They're not mortal, they could never be mortal. They could be 'untapped' if someone still wants to go the &quot;I just found out I'm adopted!&quot; route, but they're still never actually mortal.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is what I'd do if it came up in the game. If they were to be a PC I'd have them build themselves just as if they were making a new Scion for the game and then hand wave away the mortal bits. If they were an NPC their starting legend would depend on their parents and what seemed the most fun for the campaign.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Need some online tools, including a Scion/Exalted battle wheel? <a href=\"http://rpg-tools.org/generators/index.php\" target=\"_blank\">http://rpg-tools.org/generators/index.php</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/829-james-mcmurray","name":"James McMurray","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-24T19:50:05-07:00","id":291755,"title":"","content":"The only time I remember Scion bringing up the nature of children of two divine parents is in Scion: Ragnarok. The book introduces and has full write ups of additional gods and characters from Norse mythology. Among them are three of Thor's children, the twins Magni and Modi, and Thor and Sif's daughter Thrud.<br /> <br /> The write up of Magni and Modi specifically mentions that they are not nor have ever been either mortal or Scion because they don't have a mortal parent. Their mother wasn't a God though but a giantess, and the book is frustratingly vague on whether they are considered fully fledged Gods or if they are something completely different (not helped by the fact that the book points out the thin line between the Aesir and the giants and that several giants have been adopted into the pantheon and raised to Godhood). But while the book is unclear on exactly what Magni and Modi are their write up does seem to emphasize that for a Scion to be born one of the parents HAS to be mortal, any kind of union between a God and another supernatural being would create some other kind of supernatural offspring, whether that offspring is a god or something else.<br /> <br /> Thrud is the child of two Gods (Thor and Sif) and is said to be a fully fledged God but it doesn't say if she was born like that or worked her way up to Godhood. I'd probably guess she was born one since she doesn't have a mortal parent any more than Magni and Modi did and therefore according to the text from their write up she shouldn't have been born a mortal or a Scion.<br /> <br /> So I guess my interpretation is that two gods create a baby god or at least some kind of pseudo-divine offspring that may eventually be considered a God.<br /> <br /> At the very least I think a mortal parent is a necessary requirement for a child to become a Scion. <br /> But yeah, there's nothing that prevents a Storyteller from making the call that the child of two Gods is a Scion or at the very least similar enough as for there to be no mechanical difference in how you run a PC with this kind of parentage.<br /> <br /> Aynie's suggestion above seemed like a good way to do it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2030-baboonking","name":"baboonking","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2030_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-24T20:11:29-07:00","id":291784,"title":"","content":"While we're on the subject of divine parentage I was always a little curious about divine grandparents.<br /> What happens when a mortal child of a God (i.e. someone who hasn't received a Visitation yet) has children of his/her own with another mortal? Is it possible for a God's grandchild to inherit his grandparent's divine potential? Could a Visitation by the grandparent potentially turn this grandchild into a Scion? Is there a difference if the grandchild is conceived by a parent that has already been called as a Scion or if the parent is still unaware of his divine heritage?<br /> <br /> I don't remember if this was brought up in any of the books. Any ideas?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2030-baboonking","name":"baboonking","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2030_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-24T21:26:39-07:00","id":291810,"title":"","content":"I thought I read somewhere that a Scion can only produce Scions after they reach godhood. It failed to go into detail about what happens if they produce children before that occurs, but my guess is that there isn't enough divine essence within them to be passed on. It's kind of like how genetic traits have a less likelihood of being passed on the further down the bloodline you go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-25T01:21:20-07:00","id":291876,"title":"","content":"It is mentioned in the book that the child of a visited Scion can be visited by your parent.<br /> So if Sun Wukong visits his child, and that Scion has children of her own, then Sun Wukong can visit those children as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-25T11:07:33-07:00","id":292238,"title":"","content":"I thought the grand parent could visit only if the child was born to a demigod scion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/490-omenseer","name":"omenseer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar490_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"987"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-25T16:12:42-07:00","id":292492,"title":"","content":"The most relevant quote is:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Any Scion character who has become a God can procreate and conceive a Scion of his or her own. When he does so, the God passes down a divine predisposition for certain Purviews and certain Epic Attributes. These Purviews and Epic Attributes are said to be \u201cassociated with\u201d the God character, just as those aptitudes that were easiest for him to develop were associated with his own divine parent. (Until he becomes a God, his own divine parent\u2019s predispositions are passed on to any children, and they may become Scions of their divine grandparent, if they receive an appropriate Visitation.) </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-02T07:46:15-08:00","id":299994,"title":"","content":"Iry<br /> <br /> Could you give me the book and page number of that quote?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2030-baboonking","name":"baboonking","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2030_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-02T09:16:58-08:00","id":300052,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baboonking</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/291104-scion-gene-pool?p=299994#post299994\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Iry<br /> Could you give me the book and page number of that quote?</div> </div> </div> </div>Certainly, Sir. Scion: God, Page 79. A Note On Divine Heritage<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-02T14:14:49-08:00","id":300445,"title":"","content":"Thank you kindly! You are a gentleman and a scholar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2030-baboonking","name":"baboonking","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2030_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T23:38:50-08:00","id":307930,"title":"","content":"Scion companion discusses this a little in the deva section when they're bringing up the problems of having a scion whose parents are renowned for their fidelity. <br /> <br /> The game answers this by use of the Avatar birthright which allows a scion or god the ability to willingly reduce their effective legend temporarily. <br /> <br /> In this state, they can conceive mortal scions who may later accomplish something noteworthy enough to receive an official visitation (which might consist of his parents revealing that they are actually gods and their day job is fighting a cosmic war with the titans). <br /> <br /> I had one campaign with a scion of Athena who left his biological parents up to me. <br /> <br /> It was eventually revealed that his mother was Circe and his father was Ares.<br /> <br /> Circe became a rather active guide. Ares just sort of said something like &quot;oh, right. You used to be my kid.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3961-thrythlind","name":"Thrythlind","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T05:33:41-08:00","id":308090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thrythlind</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/291104-scion-gene-pool?p=307930#post307930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Scion companion discusses this a little in the deva section when they're bringing up the problems of having a scion whose parents are renowned for their fidelity. <br /> <br /> The game answers this by use of the Avatar birthright which allows a scion or god the ability to willingly reduce their effective legend temporarily. <br /> <br /> In this state, they can conceive mortal scions who may later accomplish something noteworthy enough to receive an official visitation (which might consist of his parents revealing that they are actually gods and their day job is fighting a cosmic war with the titans). <br /> <br /> I had one campaign with a scion of Athena who left his biological parents up to me. <br /> <br /> It was eventually revealed that his mother was Circe and his father was Ares.<br /> <br /> Circe became a rather active guide. Ares just sort of said something like &amp;quot;oh, right. You used to be my kid.&amp;quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>It also mentions they can sometimes conceive Scions with each other in their normal state for no reason whatsoever.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}